<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · river</p><h1 class="enc-title">Wardha River</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Wardha is a river of central <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>, rising in the Satpura range in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/madhya-pradesh">Madhya Pradesh</a> and flowing south-east for about 530 km through <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maharashtra">Maharashtra</a>, past <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ballarpur">Ballarpur</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chandrapur">Chandrapur</a>, until it joins the Wainganga to form the Pranhita. Its valley formed part of the Gond kingdom of Chanda and is rich in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/coal">coal</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/wardha-river.jpg" alt="Wardha River" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Ganesh Dhamodkar · CC BY 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Wardha_river_at_Pulgaon.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
